IQL
===

IQL asks the user for structured input: a dialog with named fields, each with a kind, a prompt, a default and constraints.
CapIql is a capability of System, used with or without a Gui:

    System:{ ... mut .iql: mut CapIql; }
    CapIql: ToIso[CapIql], WidenTo[CapIql]{ mut .ask(query: Query): mut Later[Answer]; }

.ask opens the dialog and returns at once; the Later[Answer] (LaterDesign) gets the Answer when the dialog closes.
Closing the window of the dialog without answering also gives an Answer, saying so.
An IQL dialog is a window built with the widgets of the Gui library, not a dialog of the toolkit.
An IQL dialog runs no mut code of the program: the Query is imm, and the library alone runs the window.
The IQL syntax is fluent like the Gui syntax, simpler and less flexible on purpose.

Open: the IQL syntax, Query and Answer.
Open: an IQL dialog that no code waits on any more, and one still open when the main returns: CapIql has no way to close a dialog.

The handlers
============

A handler is code of the program that a setter registers, and that the library runs as a model task of the Frame.
Each setter, with what makes its model task:

    Button.action              a click of the primary button on the Button.
    Mouse.pressed .released .moved .dragged
                               the gesture on the deepest widget with a handler for it; the gesture goes up the widget tree to it.
    Mouse.clicked .doubleClicked
                               a click: the press and the release in the same widget.
    Mouse.entered .exited      the pointer entering or leaving the widget, also when a layout or a closing menu moves the widget under the pointer.
    Widget.contextMenu         a click of the secondary button: the scope builds the menu, with its submenus.
    MenuItem.action            choosing an item without a submenu.
    Sections.onTitleClick      a click on the title of a section.
    Sections.onClose           a click on the x of a section.
    Frame.onKey                the .pressed and .released actions of a key whose first press reaches Frame.onKey,
                               and the .released actions the library runs when the focus moves, the window loses the keyboard or a new .onKey replaces the Keys.
    Frame.onClose              the close key, the close button of the desk border, Cmd+Q on macOS.
    Frame.onResize             a ResizeRequest.
    ModelFps.action            a model tick.
    _StrEditText.cleaner, _UStrEditText.cleaner, .onEdit
                               one change of the text by the user: a typed text, a text of an input method, a paste, a cut,
                               Backspace, Delete, Shift+Enter, an undo, a redo.
                               The cleaner maker makes the cleaner of the change; .onEdit runs when the text changed.
    .onCommit                  Enter, Ctrl+Enter, and the focus leaving the widget by a press elsewhere, Tab or Shift+Tab,
                               when the text differs from the committed text.

Model tasks that run no handler: a press or a drag that moves the caret, the steps of the scroll of a drag past an edge, the keys that move the caret, Ctrl+A, Ctrl+C.

Disabling a Frame
=================

    Frame:{ ...
      mut .enabled(Bool): mut Frame;
      mut .enabled: Bool;
      }
    Graphics:{ ... read .enabled: Bool; }

A Frame opens enabled.
While a Frame is disabled, each model task of the Frame is dropped instead of queued: no handler of the Frame runs, .onClose and .onResize included, and no caret moves.
.enabled(False) also drops the model tasks already queued.
The toolkit thread goes on as for an enabled Frame: the repaints, hover, tooltips, the wheel, scroll bars, the drag of a divider of a Split, the focus, the composition of an input method, the states of the window, the drag of the Fearless corner, a file dropped on the window.
A painter sees .enabled of its Graphics, and can draw a disabled look.

ImprovedGuiAPI.txt: a frame shows the result of a model task only when the task ends.
A wait inside a model task, on a Later or in CapGui.run, counts as an end: during the wait, every Frame shows the changes made before the wait.

A Frame disabled and not closed when the consumer of its CapGui.run or one of its handlers returns can never be enabled again.
Then the window closes, or does not open, and CapGui.run throws the deterministic error
`This Frame is disabled and no handler of it is running, so no code can enable it again.`

Open: a key pressed before .enabled(False) and released after it: ImprovedGuiAPI.txt says every .pressed of Frame.onKey gets exactly one .released.
Open: whether the widgets of the library draw a disabled look of their own.

A dialog in front of a Frame
============================

    .button{:: .text "Settings" .action{Block#
      .do{Block#(f.enabled False, Void)}
      .let answer= {sys.iql.ask(SettingsQuery).await}
      .do{Block#(f.enabled True, Void)}
      .return{model.apply answer}
      }}

    A The Frame f is open, with the Button above. A painter of f draws a grey cover when .enabled of its Graphics is False.
    B The user clicks Settings.
    C The IQL dialog opens, and the user observes the grey cover on f.
    D The user clicks a Button of f, types in a TextField of f, and clicks the close button of the desk border of f.
    E The user observes that f stays open and unchanged.
    F The user answers the dialog.
    G The dialog closes; the user observes f with the new settings and no grey cover.
    H The user observes that the Button of D ran no action, and that the TextField does not hold the text typed in D.
